## Findings & Notable Patterns (running log)

### Eagles (2017-2019 draft classes)
- Clean pattern of on-time extensions for homegrown talent: Barnett, Sweat,
  Goedert, and Maddox were all extended on the normal timeline.
- Jordan Mailata is the Eagles' one "extended early" case — signed before
  his year-5 window began, a more aggressive front-office bet than the rest
  of the class.
- Three players played out their full rookie deals and left via free agency
  at year 5 with no return: Miles Sanders, Andre Dillard, T.J. Edwards — all
  signed elsewhere immediately after leaving Philadelphia.
- Two players left the roster well before reaching the transition window
  (Rasul Douglas cut year 3, Ryan Bates traded before playing a single
  snap) — excluded from the core metric, but a useful "early miss" signal
  worth a footnote.
- Matt Pryor is a borderline case: traded in year 4, one year before the
  window technically starts.

### Chiefs (2017-2019 draft classes)
- Patrick Mahomes is the standout "extended early" case — signed a 10-year,
  $503M extension a full two years before his year-5 window began, on a
  scale no other player in the dataset approaches.
- Derrick Nnadi and Nick Allegretti both followed a "test free agency, then
  re-sign on a short deal" pattern at year 5 rather than a big multi-year
  extension — a different flavor of "on time" than the Eagles' bigger-money
  extensions.
- **Notable cluster:** three separate 2019 draftees — Mecole Hardman, Juan
  Thornhill, and Khalen Saunders — all hit free agency at exactly year 5 in
  the same 2023 offseason and were let go with no return. This looks like a
  deliberate front-office pattern (not extending the 2019 class) rather than
  three unrelated decisions, and is worth highlighting as a comparison point
  against how other teams handled their own draft classes.
- Tanoh Kpassagnon played out his rookie deal and left in free agency right
  at year 5 (2021) — a clean "let walk" case, unrelated to the 2019 cluster.
- Rashad Fenton was traded away in year 4 (borderline, same treatment as
  Eagles' Matt Pryor). Dorian O'Daniel and Armani Watts both left the roster
  in year 4, one year short of the window — included as borderline cases
  rather than excluded outright.

### Cross-team comparison (preliminary, 2 of 5 teams)
- Both "good" reputation teams (Eagles, Chiefs) show a mix of on-time
  extensions and clean free-agency departures — neither team appears to be
  "hoarding" talent through overpaying every player. The distinguishing
  factor so far seems to be selective aggression (Mailata, Mahomes extended
  early) rather than paying everyone.
- Worth testing once Saints/Browns data is in: do the "bad" cap management
  teams show a higher rate of *dead cap from cuts* rather than clean
  free-agency departures, versus the clean pattern seen in Eagles/Chiefs
  so far?
